`p256-wallet` and `p384-wallet` were `.inherited` until 2026-07-28 because live
`compileHex` ABORTED THE PROCESS on both (SIGABRT, exit 134, "Stack overflow
detected"), which meant the anti-paste regen backstop died on exactly the two
anchors a paste could hide behind. Root cause: `Stack/Peephole.lean`'s Phase 7.1
`applyPushOneAdd` / `applyPushOneSub` and the `postFoldList` / `applyRollPickFold`
traversals never got the tail-recursive runtime twins the other 19 peephole rules
have, so `peepholePostFold` recursed once per op. Both wallets are
single-public-method contracts, so their whole ~1.9 MB / ~3.9 MB script sits in
ONE op list and blew the 8 MB main-thread stack; the `*-primitives` fixtures emit
the same total volume across three method bodies and stayed under it, which is
why only the wallets crashed. It was deep-but-finite recursion, not a bad
algorithm: at `ulimit -s 65520` both compiled in well under a second and produced
byte-identical output. Adding the missing `.tr` twins made both re-derive at the
stock 8 MB stack, and the re-derived bytes matched the stored constants exactly —
the constants had been honest all along.
